.TH APSIS 1 "2026-10-04" "apsis 0.5.0" "User Commands"
.\" No hyphenation: action names and paths must stay whole.
.nh
.SH NAME
apsis \- system snapshot and restore for the COSMIC desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B apsis
.br
.B apsis
.B \-\-window
.br
.B apsis
.B \-\-settings
.br
.B apsis
.B \-\-about
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B apsis
takes rsync snapshots of the system onto a backup disk, lists them, deletes them, and (since
0.5.0, experimental) restores the whole system to one at the next start.
It is a COSMIC panel applet and a window, in standard COSMIC widgets that follow the theme.
.PP
Snapshots are kept in Timeshift's on\-disk layout
.RI ( timeshift/snapshots/<name>/
on the backup disk), so snapshots Timeshift made are listed and deleted like Apsis's own.
Apsis doesn't need or run Timeshift.
It is manual only: there is no schedule, and a snapshot is only deleted when the user deletes
it.
A snapshot holds the system, and
.I /root
and
.I /home
if the settings include them.
Encrypted (LUKS) backup disks aren't supported yet.
.PP
Started by the COSMIC panel,
.B apsis
is a panel button whose popup shows the last snapshot, the backup disk and the newest
snapshots, read\-only, with a button that opens the window.
Started anywhere else (the app launcher, a terminal), or with
.BR \-\-window ,
it opens the window: a toolbar (Create, Restore, Delete, Settings), the snapshot list, and a
status area with the last snapshot, the backup disk and, while a snapshot is being made or a
restore prepared, its progress and a Stop button; after a restore, how it went.
.PP
Everything that needs root (listing, creating, stopping, deleting, restoring, writing the
settings) goes through
.BR apsis\-helper ,
a small system service started on demand over D\-Bus, and polkit asks for the password.
Apsis doesn't work without it.
.SH RESTORE
.B Restore
(one snapshot selected; no keyboard shortcut) first asks the helper whether the snapshot fits
this computer: the same installation (the snapshot's root filesystem UUID), UEFI, Pop!_OS with
systemd\-boot (kernelstub), a plain ext4 system disk, no separate
.IR /boot ", " /usr " or " /var ,
the snapshot's kernel files complete, no other system update waiting for a restart, no Pop!_OS
release upgrade in progress, and the snapshot's
.I /etc/crypttab
the same as the live one.
Anything else is refused with the reason and what to do.
.PP
The dialog offers two choices: keep the home folders as they are now (the default; then
.I /home
isn't touched at all) or restore them too (then files created or changed since the snapshot are
deleted or put back), and a safety snapshot of the current system first (on by default; it
includes
.I /home
when the home folders are restored). The safety snapshot is the way back: the system as it
was right before the restore, listed as "Safety snapshot, before restoring <date>", costing
only the files that differ from the newest snapshot. To undo the restore, restore it like any
other snapshot. Without it there is no way back.
.B Restore
then prepares, with the password asked every time: the space on each disk is measured with a
dry run, the safety snapshot is taken (Stop ends it like any snapshot), the plan is written to
.IR /var/lib/apsis/restore/ ,
and a recovery note to
.I timeshift/apsis\-restore\-RECOVER.txt
on the backup disk.
.PP
.B Ready to restore
is the last word, and nothing is armed before it.
.B Restart now
checks the space and the boot files once more, arms the next start (a systemd unit in
.IR /etc/systemd/system/ ,
a copy of the helper, and the
.I /system\-update
link that puts systemd into offline\-update mode) and restarts;
.BR "Cancel restore" ,
Esc or closing the window removes the plan (a finished safety snapshot stays).
A plan left at the prompt for more than 30 minutes is refused as too old.
If the computer hasn't restarted ten minutes after Restart now, a timer disarms it.
Removing, reinstalling or upgrading the Apsis package in those minutes disarms it too, and
apt's output says so.
.PP
At the next start the restore runs with the desktop stopped (plymouth shows its progress):
rsync puts the system files back (the live
.IR /etc/fstab " and " /etc/crypttab ,
the boot partition, the recovery partition, other mounts and the kernel the computer started
with are kept), the boot partition's files are backed up, kernelstub refreshes them as
Pop!_OS's kernel hooks do, told which kernel the snapshot boots, and the result is checked
byte for byte.
If the refresh or the check fails, the computer keeps the old boot files and the kernel it
started with.
A copy that breaks (the backup disk gone, an I/O error) is tried again at the next start, three
times at most; then the restore gives up and the system may be partly restored.
Then the computer restarts normally; after login the window's status line says how it went,
with the details in its tooltip.
Once it restarts, the restore can't be stopped: don't turn off the computer until it's done.
.PP
Not restored: anything the snapshot didn't include (its own exclude list is honoured), and
.I /root
unless the snapshot has it with content.
Hard links aren't kept in snapshots, and a file changed in place with the same size and
modification time isn't restored.
See "If a restore goes wrong" in the README for the recovery steps.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-window
Open the window, even when started by the panel.
Only one window runs at a time: starting Apsis again (from the launcher or the panel's
.BR "Open Apsis" )
brings the open window forward instead.
.B COSMIC_SINGLE_INSTANCE=0
in the environment starts a separate one.
.TP
.B \-\-settings
Like
.BR \-\-window ,
on the settings.
If a window is open already, it switches to the settings, unless something is in progress
there (a snapshot it started, a dialog, unsaved settings).
.TP
.B \-\-about
Like
.BR \-\-window ,
on the About page.
.SH KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS
Everything works with the mouse; these are shortcuts in the window.
.TP
.B Ctrl+N
Create a snapshot (a dialog asks for an optional comment).
rsync runs at idle I/O priority and nice 19.
.TP
.B Delete
Delete the selected snapshots (a dialog asks first).
Several are deleted as one job, in list order, with the password asked once; it stops at the
first failure and says which were deleted and which were not.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+R ", " F5
Refresh the list.
.TP
.B Ctrl+,
Settings.
.TP
.B Ctrl+A
Select every row.
.TP
.BR Up ", " Down
Move the selection.
Ctrl\-click adds or removes a row, Shift\-click selects a range.
.TP
.B Esc
Close a dialog (on the "Ready to restore" prompt: cancel the restore), leave the settings or
About, then clear the selection.
.PP
.B Restore
has no shortcut: click it, or Tab to it and press Enter.
.SH SETTINGS
Settings has four tabs.
Location, Include and Filters are Apsis's config,
.IR /etc/apsis/config.toml ,
written by
.B apsis\-helper
on Save (password asked).
.TP
.B Location
The disk snapshots go to: a Linux filesystem, not encrypted.
Prefer another disk than the system one.
.TP
.B Include
The system is always included.
.I /root
(the root user's home folder, on by default) and
.I /home
(every user's home folder, off by default).
.TP
.B Filters
An ordered list; each line is
.B +
(keep) or
.B \-
(leave out) and an rsync pattern, and the first line that matches wins.
New lines go on top; Move Up and Move Down reorder them.
Add Folder and Add File use the file chooser (a folder becomes
.IR <folder>/*** );
Add Pattern takes a typed one, an exclude unless it starts with
.RB \(dq "+ " \(dq.
.B *
matches within one folder level,
.B **
across folders, and a leading
.B /
starts at the root.
Without it, a pattern with a
.B /
in it matches the end of any path:
.B \-\~home/Downloads
leaves out
.I /home/Downloads
and
.IR /srv/home/Downloads ,
not
.IR /home/you/Downloads .
Built\-in excludes (caches, /proc, /tmp, other mounts) always come first.
.TP
.B Misc
Per user, saved at once: the reminder (the panel icon turns the warning colour when the newest
snapshot is older than this many days; default 7, 0 turns it off) and the panel label (the
last snapshot's age and the disk use beside the icon).
.PP
Before
.I config.toml
exists, the settings show what was read from Timeshift's
.I /etc/timeshift/timeshift.json
(its backup device and filters; the file is only read), and Save keeps that as Apsis's own.
Settings from Apsis 0.3 (version 1 of the file) are shown converted and saved as version 2 on
Save; the converted settings take the same files.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/apsis/config.toml
Apsis's config: the backup device's UUID, the two includes and the filters.
Written only by
.BR apsis\-helper ,
0644, owned by root; the previous file is kept as
.IR config.toml.bak .
To go back to Apsis 0.3, copy the backup over it (as root).
.TP
.I /etc/timeshift/timeshift.json
Timeshift's settings, read once for the import while there is no
.IR config.toml ;
never written.
.TP
.I timeshift/apsis-staging/
On the backup disk: where a snapshot is built before it's moved into
.IR timeshift/snapshots/ ,
and where a delete moves a snapshot before removing it.
What an interrupted snapshot or delete leaves there is shown in the list as an unfinished row
and removed by the next snapshot (or with Delete).
A folder an older Apsis left half\-deleted in
.I timeshift/snapshots/
(an
.IR info.json ,
no
.IR exclude.list )
is such a row too; only Delete removes it.
.TP
.I timeshift/apsis\-restore\-RECOVER.txt
On the backup disk: the recovery steps with this machine's disk UUIDs and the snapshots' names
filled in, written while a restore is prepared.
.TP
.I /run/apsis/job.lock
The job lock: the helper holds a lock on it (flock) while a snapshot, a delete, a restore's
preparation or a settings save runs, and from Restart now until the next start is armed.
The package's scripts take it too, so removing, reinstalling or upgrading the Apsis package
is refused while a job runs: apt stops with "apsis: an Apsis job is running; try again when
it has finished", the package stays as it was and the job goes on.
At the Ready to restore prompt the package operation goes on and the preparation is gone.
The file is made again after each restart; its being there means nothing.
.TP
.I /var/lib/apsis/restore/
The restore's state folder (root only): the plan
.RI ( request.json ),
the rsync filter and the recovery note while a restore is prepared
.RI ( restore.filter ,
.IR restore.note ),
the two kept when it was last armed, which the recovery steps use
.RI ( last\-restore.filter ,
.IR last\-restore.note ),
rsync's log, the attempt counter
.RI ( state.json ),
the boot partition's backup
.RI ( esp\-backup/ ),
the last result
.RI ( result.json ,
what the window shows after login) and, while a restore is armed, a copy of the helper.
Removed on purge.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/apsis\-restore.service
.TQ
.I /etc/systemd/system/system\-update.target.wants/apsis\-restore.service
.TQ
.I /etc/systemd/system/pop\-upgrade\-init.service.d/50\-apsis.conf
.TQ
.I /system\-update
Written when a restore is armed and removed when it ends, when it's disarmed, and when the
package is removed (the drop\-in keeps Pop!_OS's release
upgrade from running in the restore's start; the link is what makes the next start an
offline update).
A restore that won't end: boot the recovery and remove
.I /system\-update
(README, "If a restore goes wrong").
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/apsis-helper
The privileged helper.
.B apsis\-helper \-\-apply\-restore
is what the armed unit runs at the next start, and
.B apsis\-helper \-\-disarm
what the ten\-minute timer and the package's removal script run; neither is for hand use.
.TP
.I /usr/share/dbus-1/system-services/io.github.atraxsrc.Apsis.Helper.service
.TQ
.I /usr/share/dbus-1/system.d/io.github.atraxsrc.Apsis.Helper.conf
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/apsis-helper.service
D\-Bus activation, bus policy and systemd unit of the helper.
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/io.github.atraxsrc.Apsis.policy
The polkit actions below.
.SH SECURITY
The applet runs as the user and never as root.
.B apsis\-helper
checks each call with polkit, against the caller's D\-Bus name, and runs one write (a create,
a delete, a settings save, a restore's preparation) at a time; lists share a read\-only mount
and run at the same time as each other, and a write waits for them (up to 15 seconds) before it
mounts read\-write.
While a restore plan waits at its prompt, every write is refused as busy and lists go on.
A write also holds the job lock
.RI ( /run/apsis/job.lock ),
which the package's removal script takes without waiting: a package operation is refused
while a job runs, and a job is refused as busy while a package operation holds the lock.
It mounts the backup disk (by UUID, nosuid, nodev) for the length of the call, read\-only
except for a create or a delete.
A delete only removes a folder named like a snapshot under
.I timeshift/snapshots/
(or
.IR timeshift/apsis-staging/ ),
reached without any symlink and with nothing mounted inside it; it never follows a symlink or
crosses into another filesystem.
It first moves the snapshot into
.I timeshift/apsis-staging/
(never over a folder already there), so a delete cut off part-way leaves an unfinished row,
not a half\-removed snapshot.
Stopping a snapshot ends rsync's process group (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 10 seconds) and
removes what it copied the same way.
Calls and results are logged to the journal
.RB ( "journalctl \-u apsis\-helper" ).
For the active session:
.TP
.B io.github.atraxsrc.Apsis.list
Listing snapshots, reading the settings and asking what the helper is doing: no password.
.TP
.B io.github.atraxsrc.Apsis.create
.TQ
.B io.github.atraxsrc.Apsis.delete
.TQ
.B io.github.atraxsrc.Apsis.configure
Creating, deleting and writing the settings: the password, remembered for a few minutes
.RB ( auth_admin_keep ).
.TP
.B io.github.atraxsrc.Apsis.stop
Stopping a snapshot another user started (the user who started it isn't asked): the password,
remembered for a few minutes.
.TP
.B io.github.atraxsrc.Apsis.restore
Preparing a restore: the password, asked every time
.RB ( auth_admin ).
Restart now and Cancel restore aren't asked of the user who prepared the plan.
.PP
Inactive and remote sessions need the password for every action.
